tegra: ORIN-GLASSINK — the ink census names what it saw, so INK-NO-STEM stops hiding three answers

boot7j read `paper=1001 ink=23 stem=0 -> INK-NO-STEM` stably, with all six chrome
probes MATCH and onpanel=yes, and that verdict was read as "the text is not on the
glass". It does not support that reading: `ink` was "not exactly LAD_PAPER" and
`stem` was "exactly LAD_INK", so the pair says only that 23 samples were neither
exact black nor exact light-grey. Anti-aliased text of this console's own colour,
text in some other colour, and a foreign surface all produce it. The probe reported
`first=` (the first sample, which was paper) and never what the 23 ink samples were.

The census now classifies every non-paper sample by geometry in colour space against
the two documented constants as VARIABLES: `lad_classify` recovers the coverage from
the widest paper-to-ink channel span and requires the other two to agree within
LAD_BLEND_TOL, so `blend` counts samples on the PAPER-to-INK segment and `off`
counts the rest. A fixed six-slot Misra-Gries table (no allocation, 48 bytes of
stack) carries the heaviest non-paper values to the wire as ink1..ink3/n1..n3, with
inkvals/exact stating whether the counts are exact or lower bounds.

Three verdicts replace the one bucket: a blend supermajority is positive
identification of this console's own anti-aliased text and PASSES as
GLYPHS-AA-ON-GLASS / GLYPHS-AA-NO-CHROME; a single off-ramp value holding a majority
of the ink is INK-OFF-COLOUR with the measured colour named; the residue keeps
INK-NO-STEM with its documented meaning. LAD_INK is NOT changed to match the board -
a constant tuned to the observation would agree by construction and prove nothing.

Rung (b)'s `painted` derivation now goes through lad_glass_painted, the one place the
passing set is written down, so an anti-aliased restore cannot read as
FAIL reason=restore-blank.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
